    756:| Free cumulants of the chi_P MPS unfolding operator — composition C2 of E2.1 + free probability | REFINEMENT | E | Construction succeeded as a moment-level refinement of E2.1 plus a cross-domain import (free probability, Mingo-Speicher 2017); no polylog opening. Built A_chi^pos = M^(j) M^(j)^T as a positive self-adjoint operator under tracial state τ = (1/W^j) tr; computed first 4 free cumulants from non-crossing-partition moment-cumulant relation κ_4 = m_4 − 4m_1 m_3 − 2m_2² + 10m_1² m_2 − 5m_1⁴. Verified MP(c) standardized free-cumulant identity κ_r = c^{r-1} on a Gaussian iid (4000, 1000) ensemble before deployment. Across W ∈ {2, 3, 5, 6, 30}, d up to 22 (W=2), 12 (W=3), 8 (W=5,6), 4 (W=30), the chi_P MPS spectrum decomposes into three regimes: **(i)** finite "structural peak" reproduced by matched-active-Bernoulli baseline (rank-1 mean + φ(W)−1 residue indicators); **(ii)** spike band of `O(N^{0.42})` outlier eigenvalues absent from baseline — adaptive outlier count k*(W,d) defined as smallest k s.t. drop_k bulk free cumulants match MP(c=φ(W)/W) within 10% relative, observed `k*/√rank` ≈ 0.6–1.6 (drifting upward from 0.62 at W=2 d=14 to 1.56 at W=2 d=22; fitting `k* ∝ R^{0.85}`); **(iii)** MP bulk matching `c = φ(W)/W = ∏_{p≤W}(1 − 1/p)` (the **Mertens product**) exactly. Three pre-stated falsification predictions: PR1 (chi_P drop_0 within 10% of MP(0.5)) FAIL at small drop, PASS after adaptive drop; PR2 (active-Bernoulli matches MP) PASS from drop_1; PR3 (chi_P bulk matches MP after finite projection across all W) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) the MP-bulk free-Poisson rate of chi_P's MPS unfolding equals the Mertens product φ(W)/W — a new identification of a free-probabilistic invariant with a sieve density; (b) spike-count regularity `k*(W,d) ∝ R^{0.85}` — a structural fact beyond E2.1's rank statement; (c) verified MP(c) standardized cumulant identity. **Algorithmic implication**: any spectral compression of M^(j) faithful at the second-moment level (i.e., reproducing κ_2) needs rank Ω(N^{0.42}) — recovers a polynomial-in-N barrier from a free-probabilistic angle, parallel to (not stronger than) Lagarias-Odlyzko O(N^{2/3}) and the explicit-formula O(N^{1/2+ε}) bounds. Cites E2.1; cross-domain reference Mingo & Speicher 2017 Ch. 1-2, Ch. 4. See experiments/constructions/free_cumulants_chi_p/. | 74 |
    757-| §A.A3 ATTACK_VECTORS — spectral primality test via fixed-generator (Z/nZ)* Cayley graph (cross-domain: spectral graph theory, Bourgain) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether spec(Cay((Z/nZ)*, {2,3,5,2⁻¹,3⁻¹,5⁻¹})) decides primality of n. Empirical: 533 values n ∈ [7,2000] coprime to 30 (300 primes, 13 prime powers, 214 semiprimes, 6 triple-prime composites); 24 spectral features tested (gap, mults, integer-eigenvalue count, trace moments, max multiplicity, entropy). **No feature is disjoint primes-vs-composites.** Mann-Whitney AUC for primes-vs-prime-powers (the hard sub-case): 0.509 with {2,3,5}, 0.566 with {2,3,7}, 0.673 with {2,3,5,7}∪inv — essentially noise. **Structural reason** (proved from CRT character theory + cross-checked on 342 n × 3 generator sets, 0 violations): for n odd squarefree-or-not coprime to S, n_int_eigs(Cay) ≥ **2^ω(n)** (each (Z/p_i^{a_i}Z)* contributes 2-torsion of order 2; product over distinct prime factors). Lower bound is sharp in 84/533 cases. **Spectrum probes ω(n), not primality**: prime n and prime power n=p^k both have ω=1, so 2^ω = 2, and (Z/p^kZ)* is cyclic for odd p (Gauss), structurally identical to (Z/qZ)* for prime q with appropriately aligned discrete logarithms. (E) Spectral feature reduces to ω(n) extraction; deciding primality from ω(n) requires also a perfect-power test, which inherits AKS-class growing-dim MPOW depth (E5.3, E7.10). **Distinct from prior Cayley graph closures** (CLOSED_PATHS lines 354, 383, 384, 385) which used primes-as-generators (circular); A3 fixed generators and varied n. **Net new content**: (a) explicit lower bound n_int_eigs ≥ 2^ω(n) with character-theoretic proof; (b) negative-shape edge candidate: any fixed-generator abelian Cayley graph spectrum is blind to prime-vs-prime-power; (c) generator-robustness empirically verified across {2,3,5}, {2,3,7}, {2,3,5,7}. Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10. See experiments/circuit_complexity/cayley_spectral_primality/. | 79 |
    758-| Tensor-network compression family (MPS / HT / TR / PEPS / CP / Tucker / MERA) — N1 negative-shape closure of E2.1 | REFINEMENT | I | Family-level lift of E2.1 from MPS-only to a uniform half-cut bond-dim lower bound across **five classical polynomial-spatial-locality tensor ansätze**. For each (W, d) pair tested (22 pairs spanning W∈{2,3,4,5,6,7}, d∈{4,6,8,10,12}), measured: (a) MPS half-cut bond dim (E2.1 verification); (b) Hierarchical Tucker root-children bond dim; (c) PEPS 2D-reshape boundary cut rank; (d) CP-rank Kruskal lower bound = max over j of unfolding rank; (e) Tensor Ring bond dim (structurally MPS). **Empirical finding**: all four bond-dim measures are *identical* and equal `min(W^j, φ(W)·W^(d-j-1)+1)` at the maximising cut j=d/2, exactly in 21/22 cases (the one finite-size deficit is W=5 d=4: actual 20 vs predicted 21, a single-row dependency at small N=625). Asymptotic ratio `bond_dim / sqrt(N) → φ(W)/W` (the Mertens product) verified at d=12 for W∈{2,3,4} and at d=8 for W=6: 33/64=0.515 vs 1/2; 487/729=0.668 vs 2/3; 513/1024=0.501 vs 1/2; 433/1296=0.334 vs 1/3. Tucker and MERA close by **orthogonal mechanisms**: Tucker by mode-slice independence (Dirichlet's theorem forces multilinear rank tuple = (W,W,...,W), no compression); MERA by parameter-count (depth log d × bond dim χ^c can't represent N^{1/2} unfolding rank without polynomial χ). **Unifying mechanism**: each ansatz admits an unfolding contraction whose matricisation rank IS the bond / rank parameter; this matricisation rank is bounded below by E2.1. **No falsification fires** on F1-F6 from definition.md. **Net new content**: (i) E2.1 promoted from single-ansatz (MPS) to family-level (5 ansätze + 2 by orthogonal closure); (ii) the Mertens product φ(W)/W is the universal asymptotic compression ratio across the family; (iii) family-level closure subsumes prior single-decomposition CLOSED_PATHS rows (lines 171, 185, 517, 518, 600). **Algorithmic implication**: no polynomial-spatial-locality tensor-network ansatz on chi_P admits polylog bond dim — generalises the Lagarias-Odlyzko sqrt(x) barrier from MPS to all listed ansätze. Cites E2.1 (annotated S77), E1.9, E6.3. See experiments/constructions/tensor_compression_family_closure/. | 77 |
    759-| §D.D4 ATTACK_VECTORS — Szegedy quantum walks on number-theoretic graphs (cross-domain: Szegedy 2004 discriminant matrix theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether Szegedy walks on a number-theoretic graph yield polylog primality extraction. Tested **three families** with the discriminant matrix `D(x,y) = sqrt(P(x,y)·P(y,x))` and the eigenvalue-lifting theorem `cos(θ_k) → e^{±2iθ_k}` (mixing time `O(1/sqrt(δ))`). **(A) Cayley `Cay((Z/NZ)*, {2,3,5,inv})`** for primes N∈{31,…,1009} (14 sizes): empirical `t_classical(N) ~ N^{0.789}`, `t_szegedy(N) ~ N^{0.414}` — quadratic Szegedy speedup empirically realised but neither is polylog. Direct **quantum extension of E7.12 (S79)**: classical Cayley spectrum probes ω(n) not primality, AND Szegedy quantisation inherits the same poly-mixing barrier. **(B) Coprime `C_x = ([1..x], gcd=1)`** for x∈{30,…,1000}: spectral gap is `Ω(1)` (asymptotically `δ → 0.4166...`); stationary prime mass / uniform prime mass `→ ζ(2) = π²/6 ≈ 1.6449` (Mertens; verified at x=1000 with deviation -0.022). Both classical and Szegedy mix in `O(1)`, but bias is constant — `O(log x)` samples to hit a prime + AKS verification = no polylog extractor of π(x). **(C) Divisor `D_x = ([1..x], m|n)`**: gap `~Ω(1)` (slowly decaying 0.21 → 0.166 across x=30..1000). High-prime-mass eigenvectors localise on **prime-centered divisor clusters** `{p, 2p, 3p, ..., 1}` for *individual* primes near x/3-x/2 (e.g., k=14 eigenvector at x=250 is concentrated 100% on {43, 47} ∪ multiples ∪ {1}). Each eigenvector identifies 1-2 specific primes — same structural mechanism as E7.12 (degree class probing), not global primality. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: empirical confirmation `t_Q ≈ sqrt(t_C)` for the Szegedy-Cayley case validates the discriminant theorem at the project's scale; the π²/6 stationary identity is a clean Mertens-law derivation specific to the coprime-graph chain. **Falsification check**: no graph yields mixing time `O(polylog(x))` AND a primality-localised eigenvector with poly-log gap; both Cayley (poly mixing) and coprime/divisor (Ω(1) mixing but no global primality eigenvector) miss the joint condition. Closes ATTACK_VECTORS §D.D4. **Suggested EDGE** (E7.13 candidate): "Szegedy-walk quantisation of any reversible chain on `[1..x]` with adjacency defined by simple arithmetic relation does not yield polylog primality extraction — either spectral gap shrinks polynomially (Cayley class) or eigenvectors capture primality only via degree-class clustering (coprime / divisor class). The two conditions are exhaustive on the families tested." Cites E5.3, E5.8, E7.10, E7.12. Cross-domain reference: Szegedy 2004 (arxiv quant-ph/0401053). See experiments/quantum/szegedy_walk_prime_graphs/. | 80 |
    760-| C6 (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES) — three-pillars × HKM time-space tradeoff classification | REFINEMENT | I | Composition of E7.7 (three-pillars informational closure: prime positions / zeta zeros / floor values) and E6.7 (HKM at (T = N^{8/15}, S = N^{1/3})). Built (alpha, beta) catalog of 14 algorithms (4 prime / 4 zeta / 6 floor) and computed per-pillar Pareto frontiers, cross-pillar dominance, and saturation against E7.6 lower bound (T*S ≥ Ω(N^{5/6}/log N)). **Findings**: (i) HKM is on the floor-pillar Pareto frontier and DOMINATES every other floor-pillar entry elementwise (Lucy DP, Lucy+Fenwick, Meissel-Lehmer classical, Gourdon, primecount); (ii) HKM has NO cross-pillar dominators — its (8/15, 1/3) point is uniquely accessible on the floor pillar (no zero-pillar or prime-pillar entry achieves both T ≤ N^{8/15} AND S ≤ N^{1/3}); (iii) **pillar dominance regions are non-overlapping**: time-only minimum is shared by prime/zeta pillars at α=1/2, space-only minimum is unique to floor at β=1/3, T*S minimum is unique to floor at 13/15 ≈ 0.867; (iv) HKM saturates E7.6 to within N^{0.034} (gap 13/15 - 5/6 = 1/30); (v) Aggarwal binary search (E6.6) is a meta-algorithm whose effective placement migrates with the chosen pi(x) sub-routine. All four pre-stated falsification criteria (F1: HKM dominated; F2: HKM cross-pillar dominated; F3: identical pillar frontiers; F4: floor-pillar T*S < 5/6) PASS. **Net new content**: (a) explicit pillar-by-pillar Pareto map of the project's catalog; (b) HKM uniqueness lemma (HKM's (T, S) point is achievable on the floor pillar ONLY); (c) saturation verdict (floor pillar asymptotically tight). No polylog opening — refinement of E7.7 and E6.7, not a new edge. Cites E5.1, E6.5, E6.6, E6.7, E6.8, E6.9, E7.6, E7.7, E7.9. See experiments/constructions/pillar_tradeoff_diagram/. | 81 |
